<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Juthungi</h1><p class="st-meta">260 CE – 430 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic people north of the Danube in Bavaria and Franconia who raided Roman Italy in the 3rd century and were later absorbed into the Alemanni.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Juthungi were probably a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a> who settled north of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetian-limes">Raetian Limes</a>. They first appear around 260; an altar found at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1992 records a Roman victory over 'Semnones or Iouthungi' who were returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with thousands of captives.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their society was led by warrior elites capable of mounting long-range raids. In 270 they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dexippus">Dexippus</a> preserves a speech of their envoys to Emperor Aurelian claiming 40,000 horsemen. Aurelian defeated them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later recruited Juthungi as soldiers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Juthungi raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a> again in the 350s and 383, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> fought them in 429 to 430, their last mention. They are generally thought to have merged with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni</a>, whose Bavarian and Franconian neighbours inherited their land.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/juthungi.jpg" alt="Juthungi" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Photographed by User:Bullenwächter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburger_Weihestein_l.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>260 CE – 430 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elbe Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iuthungi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=345&amp;focus=juthungi" aria-label="Juthungi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4907 395 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Juthungi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5227 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5227 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=345&amp;focus=juthungi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
